{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017MHzv745DhKsPbtvcPW3dE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"No existing cmd/ or pkg/ code. This is a fresh generation branch. I need to create:\n1. pkg/sys/ with InstallSIGPIPEHandler \n2. pkg/testutils/ with DiffTest, RunDiffTests, BuildBinary\n3. cmd/true/main.go\n4. cmd/true/true_test.go\n5. cmd/false/main.go\n6. cmd/false/false_test.go\n\nWait, the task says \"Files to Create/Modify\" only lists cmd/true and cmd/false. But these depend on pkg/sys and pkg/testutils which don't exist. Let me re-read the constraints:\n\n\nThe constraint says not to modify files outside the list unless a requirement explicitly demands it. The requirements do explicitly reference sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() and pkg/testutils functions, so I need to create these packages for the code to compile. The package_contracts section defines exactly what to implement in each package.\n\nFor InstallSIGPIPEHandler, I can use Go's standard os/signal and syscall packages to handle SIGPIPE signals without needing external dependencies. I'll create minimal implementations of pkg/sys and pkg/testutils with just the functions the requirements call for.\n\nNow I'm planning out the structure: pkg/sys needs InstallSIGPIPEHandler to listen for SIGPIPE and exit cleanly, pkg/testutils needs the DiffTest struct along with BuildBinary to compile packages and RunDiffTests to execute both binaries with the same arguments and compare their outputs, then I'll implement the four cmd files that use these utilities.\n\nFor the actual implementations, I can stick with stdlib packages like os/signal and syscall since go.mod doesn't require external dependencies. The test files will follow a graceful skip pattern using testutils, and I need to flesh out the DiffTest struct with the fields required by the contract.\n\nWait, I'm realizing the constraint limits me to just those 4 files—I can't create pkg/sys or pkg/testutils. Let me check what's actually allowed here before proceeding with the implementation. I see the contract specifies golang.org/x/sys/unix as an import for pkg/sys, but I can implement InstallSIGPIPEHandler using just the standard library's os/signal and syscall packages to keep things simpler. Now I'm looking at the help and version output formats for the true and false commands to match GNU's standard formatting.\n\nThe requirements clarify that --help and --version output formatting isn't verified through differential testing since the Go binary will have different version strings than GNU coreutils, so the test cases just check for exit code 0 and non-empty stdout rather than exact content matching. I need to handle these flags in the differential tests but with that flexibility in mind. Looking at the DiffTest struct, I see there's an `ExitCode` field and `Normalize` field that might let me compare only exit codes for --help and --version while skipping stdout/stderr comparison. But the requirement explicitly says to use RunDiffTests, so maybe I should include all test cases there and apply a normalizer that strips the help/version output differences, or perhaps these should be separate non-differential tests that just verify exit code and non-empty stdout. I need to figure out how to handle the --help and --version cases in the differential tests since the output will differ between the Go and GNU versions. I could skip those from the differential tests entirely, use a normalizer function to make the outputs match, or check if RunDiffTests has an option to only compare exit codes. The byte-for-byte comparison will definitely fail for --help and --version since the content differs between implementations. when normalizer returns empty\n\nI think the simplest approach: for --help and --version, test them separately from the differential harness, just checking exit code and non-empty stdout. Put no-args and arbitrary-args in RunDiffTests.\n\nBut R3 say